// An expression tree, evaluated by walking itself.
//
// A node holds its children in a `series of expr` — a series is a reference, so the struct
// closes and the type is finite. Holding a child BY VALUE cannot work and the compiler says
// so: `the expr left` would be a node containing a node containing a node, with no end and
// no fixed size. The indirection is what makes a recursive shape possible, and a container
// is how Cufet spells it.
//
// This program used to encode the same tree as four parallel series with -1 for "no child".
// That worked, but it is the workaround, not the shape.

Define object expr with (the text kind, the number value, the series of expr kids).

Pull a rabbit.
    Bind number to eval, given (the expr e):
        If the kind of e is "num", return the value of e.
        Define lv as Cast eval on (item 1 of the kids of e).
        Define rv as Cast eval on (item 2 of the kids of e).
        If the kind of e is "add", return lv + rv.
        If the kind of e is "mul", return lv * rv.
        Return 0.
    Done.

    // A leaf carries a value and no children; a branch carries children and no value.
    Define two   as a new expr { the kind "num", the value 2, the kids a series of expr }.
    Define three as a new expr { the kind "num", the value 3, the kids a series of expr }.
    Define four  as a new expr { the kind "num", the value 4, the kids a series of expr }.

    // Built leaves-first, because a branch needs its children to exist before it can hold them.
    Define product as a new expr { the kind "mul", the value 0, the kids a series of expr with (three, four) }.
    Define sum     as a new expr { the kind "add", the value 0, the kids a series of expr with (two, product) }.

    Define result as Cast eval on (sum).
    State "2 + 3 * 4 = {result}".
Done.
